                <h1>Terms of Service</h1>
                <p class="doc-meta">Effective 19 September 2026 &middot; SACC Software Limited</p>
            </header>

            <p class="doc-lede">
                This is the agreement between you (or the organization you're signing up on behalf of) and SACC
                Software Limited for the use of Q-Mgr. Registering an organization, or clicking "I agree" on the
                sign-up form, means you're accepting it — so it's worth actually reading, and we've tried to
                write it so that's not a chore.
            </p>

            <h2>1. Who can use Q-Mgr</h2>
            <p>
                You need to be at least 18 and have real authority to sign your organization up — a business
                owner, a head teacher, an IT admin, an operations manager, whoever's actually responsible for
                making this call. If you're setting this up on behalf of an organization, "you" throughout this
                document means that organization, and you're confirming you're allowed to bind it to these terms.
            </p>

            <h2>2. What Q-Mgr actually is</h2>
            <p>
                Q-Mgr is a front-office platform made of modules. Your organization chooses which ones it uses:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Core Queue Management</strong> &mdash; ticket queues across service counters, the self-service kiosk and the customer display.</li>
                <li><strong>Visitor Management</strong> &mdash; front-desk check-in and check-out, badges, pre-registered arrivals, a security watchlist and the evacuation roll-call.</li>
                <li><strong>Communication</strong> &mdash; digital signage, Broadcasts (SMS, email, WhatsApp and Telegram messages to your own contacts), customer feedback and surveys, and a document Library with secure sharing links.</li>
                <li><strong>Welfare &amp; Performance</strong> &mdash; for schools: the student roster and guardians, visiting-day passes and the welfare ledger, and staff performance &mdash; duties and registers, the timetable, recognition, appraisals and each staff member's own workspace.</li>
                <li><strong>Integrations &amp; API</strong> &mdash; API access and partner integrations.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Every organization gets its own isolated space — your data, your branches, your users — even
                though everyone runs on the same platform underneath.
            </p>

            <h2>3. Your account and your trial</h2>
            <p>
                New organizations can try a module free for a trial period, with no payment details needed up
                front &mdash; the current trial length is shown when you sign up, since it's the kind of number
                that changes more often than this document should. A module on trial has to be paid for before
                you add another one. If a trial ends without payment, that module stops working; your data stays
                put, and paying for the module brings it straight back. You're responsible for keeping your login
                credentials to yourself and for what happens under your account, so use a real password and
                don't share logins between staff who should each have their own.
            </p>
            <p>
                If a trial ends and is never paid for, the account doesn't sit here for ever: it follows the deletion
                timetable in Section 8a, and after 104 days everything in it is permanently deleted. We'll email you
                before each step.
            </p>

            <h2>4. Prices, paying, and cancelling</h2>
            <p>
                You pay per module, monthly or annually, in advance, in Uganda shillings. Current prices are
                shown on the Billing page inside the app. The price you agree to when you add a module is the
                price you keep paying for it: if our list price changes later, your renewals stay at the price
                you agreed unless we tell you otherwise in advance.
            </p>
            <p>
                Payments are collected through the SACC payment gateway (sacc.ug), by Mobile Money (MTN or
                Airtel) or by card where card payments are available. A Mobile Money payment is only ever taken
                after you approve the prompt on your phone with your own PIN; a card is entered on the gateway's
                secure payment page, never on Q-Mgr. When a module renews, we send a Mobile Money prompt for the
                amount due to the renewal number your organization has set on the Billing page, and you can change
                that number there at any time. An invoice is marked paid only once the payment has actually been
                confirmed, and a receipt is sent by email.
            </p>
            <p>
                If a renewal isn't paid, the module becomes overdue, and if it stays unpaid past a grace period
                we'll suspend the account rather than let usage run up against an unpaid bill — you'll see exactly
                why, with a way to pay, not just a dead login page. You can pay an open invoice yourself from the
                Billing page at any time.
            </p>
            <p>
                You can remove a module whenever you like from the Billing page. Removing it ends access to that
                module straight away, and you can add it back later. We don't refund unused time unless something
                on our end genuinely went wrong, or you were charged an amount you didn't agree to.
            </p>

            <h2>5. Using it responsibly</h2>
            <p>You agree not to:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Use Q-Mgr for anything illegal, or to harass, defraud, or discriminate against anyone.</li>
                <li>Send Broadcasts to people who haven't given you a real reason to expect them — a past customer, a patient, a parent, someone who opted in. The unsubscribe link on every message is there so recipients can opt out; removing it, ignoring opt-outs, or buying a contact list to blast is a violation of these terms and, in most places, the law. That's on you as the sender, not on us.</li>
                <li>Use the Visitor Management watchlist to flag people for discriminatory reasons. It exists for genuine security purposes — a documented past incident, a real safety concern — not as a way to informally blacklist someone you'd rather not deal with.</li>
                <li>Record anything in the welfare ledger or a staff member's file that you have no genuine reason to record, or give staff access to children's records they don't need to do their job. Who can see what is under your control, through the roles and visibility levels you set.</li>
                <li>Try to break the isolation between organizations, access another organization's data, or probe the platform for vulnerabilities without telling us first (we'd rather you email us about a security issue than exploit it — see Section 10).</li>
                <li>Resell or sublicense access to the platform itself without a separate written agreement with us.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                If we find an account doing any of the above, we can suspend or terminate it. We'd rather warn
                you and give you a chance to fix it — abuse serious enough for an instant termination is the
                exception, not our default move.
            </p>

            <h2>6. Whose data it is</h2>
            <p>
                Everything you or your staff put into Q-Mgr — your visitor logs, your queue history, your
                students' and staff records, your documents, your contact lists, your branding — is yours. We
                don't claim ownership of it, and we don't use it for anything beyond running the service you're
                paying us for and, in aggregate and de-identified form, understanding how the platform gets used
                so we can improve it. You are responsible for having a lawful basis for the personal data you put
                in, and for the people you let see it. If your account is closed, your data is kept for a short
                recovery window and then deleted, following the retention rules in our Privacy Policy.
            </p>

            <h2>7. Uptime and things breaking</h2>
            <p>
                We run Q-Mgr to be available and reliable, and we take outages seriously when they happen. We're
                not going to promise a specific uptime percentage with financial penalties attached here — a
                formal service-level agreement is something a customer can discuss with us directly, not a
                blanket claim we'll make to everyone up front. What we will say plainly: if the service is down,
                we're already working on it, and we'll tell you what happened once we know.
            </p>

            <h2>8. Ending the relationship</h2>
            <p>
                You can close your account whenever you like by removing its modules from the Billing page, or by
                emailing us. We can suspend or terminate an account for non-payment, a real breach of Section 5,
                or if we're required to by law — and where it's practical to do so without making the underlying
                problem worse, we'll give notice and a chance to fix things first. On termination, access stops,
                and your data follows the deletion timetable in Section 8a rather than disappearing instantly.
            </p>

            <h2>8a. If you stop paying, your data is eventually deleted in full</h2>
            <p>
                We don't keep unpaid accounts indefinitely. If an account stops being paid for &mdash; a trial that
                ended, or a subscription that lapsed &mdash; it moves through a published timetable, and at the end
                of it everything we hold for that organization is <strong>permanently and irreversibly deleted</strong>.
                Each step is measured from the one before it, and we email the address on the account before each one:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Suspended</strong> &mdash; nobody can sign in. Your data is untouched. Paying brings it
                    straight back. After <strong>60 days</strong> the account is closed.</li>
                <li><strong>Closed</strong> &mdash; the account is over and no further charges are made. Your data is
                    still here and reactivating still recovers it. After <strong>30 days</strong> it is scheduled for
                    deletion.</li>
                <li><strong>Scheduled for deletion</strong> &mdash; the last window, and the last chance. After
                    <strong>14 days</strong> the deletion happens.</li>
                <li><strong>Deleted</strong> &mdash; irreversible. There is no undo, and no copy we can go back to.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                That is <strong>104 days</strong> from the day an account stops being paid for to the day its data is
                gone, with three chances to come back. A signed-up account that never verifies its email address is
                suspended after 14 days and then follows the same path.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>What "deleted" means here.</strong> Every record belonging to your organization &mdash; students,
                staff, visitors, welfare records, documents, messages, settings and every uploaded file &mdash; is removed
                from our systems, and we check afterwards that nothing was left behind. Your backups are a separate matter
                and we won't pretend otherwise: deleted data can still exist in encrypted database backups until those
                backups expire on their normal schedule, which is <strong>30 days</strong>. It is not used for anything in
                that window, and if a backup is ever restored the deletion is applied again automatically so nothing comes
                back. After 30 days no copy remains anywhere.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>The two things we keep, and why.</strong> First, if you ever paid us, Uganda's Tax Procedures Code
                requires us to keep the tax record of that payment for five years; we keep the invoice and the payment
                &mdash; the amount, the date, the reference &mdash; and we delete the contact details attached to them.
                If you never paid us, there is no such record and nothing is kept. Second, we keep a small marker showing
                that an account was deleted and when: it contains no name, no address and no email address, only one-way
                codes that cannot be turned back into anybody. It exists so a deleted account cannot quietly reappear,
                and so we can answer honestly if you ask us later what happened to your data.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>You can ask us to delete it sooner.</strong> Email us and we'll bring the deletion forward rather
                than making you wait out the timetable. You can also ask for a copy of your data before it goes, at any
                point before the final step.
            </p>

            <h2>9. Liability</h2>
            <p>
                Q-Mgr is provided as-is. We work to keep it accurate and available, but we can't promise it will
                be error-free or uninterrupted, and to the extent the law allows, we're not liable for indirect
                or consequential losses — lost business, lost data from something outside our control, that sort
                of thing. Nothing here limits liability that can't legally be limited, like liability for our own
                gross negligence or willful misconduct.
            </p>

            <h2>10. Found a security issue?</h2>
            <p>
                Tell us before you tell anyone else: <a href="mailto:support@getsacc.com">support@getsacc.com</a>.
                We won't pursue action against a good-faith report made responsibly, and we genuinely want to
                know before a real problem finds us the hard way.
            </p>

            <h2>11. Changes to these terms</h2>
            <p>
                If we change these terms in a way that meaningfully affects how you use Q-Mgr, we'll update the
                effective date above and email account admins directly. Continuing to use the service after that
                means you've accepted the update; if you don't agree with a change, you can close your account
                before it takes effect.
            </p>

            <h2>12. Governing law</h2>
            <p>
                These terms are governed by the laws of the Republic of Uganda. Any dispute that can't be worked
                out directly between us will be handled under that jurisdiction.
            </p>

            <h2>13. Contact</h2>
            <p>
                Questions about any of this: <a href="mailto:support@getsacc.com">support@getsacc.com</a>.
            </p>

